WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:05.060
The newest trailer for Avengers: Endgame hit
in the early minutes of Super Bowl LIII.

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:09.180
And for most fans watching what turned out
to be one of the more mediocre Big Games in

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:13.120
recent memory, it was probably one of the
few actual highlights of the night.

00:00:13.450 --> 00:00:15.980
The only real complaint we have about the
30 second spot?

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:17.460
It just wasn't long enough.

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:21.700
Still, with such a short running time, the
trailer was packed with details and moments

00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:23.620
that seemed to hide plenty of significance.

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:27.480
While we can't know for sure what any of these
scenes might truly mean until the movie comes

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:32.360
out in late-April, we can definitely make
some educated — or not-so-educated — guesses.

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:36.380
Here are some of the small details in the
Big Game's Avengers: Endgame trailer that

00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:37.320
you might've missed.

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:38.980
Potential spoilers ahead.

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:43.080
First, the trailer runs through ten shots
of MCU characters.

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:44.880
Who's in this blink-and-you'll-miss-it lineup?

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:49.820
Starting at the top, we see the Wasp; Nick
Fury; Spider-Man; Doctor Strange; Falcon;

00:00:49.829 --> 00:00:55.260
Scarlet Witch; Star-Lord, Groot, Drax, Gamora,
and Rocket; Black Panther and a couple members

00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:58.300
of the Dora Milaje; the Winter Soldier; and
the Vision.

00:00:58.549 --> 00:01:02.680
With the exception of Rocket, and maybe those
two guards sharing the scene with T'Challa,

00:01:02.690 --> 00:01:07.250
all of these characters were killed in Infinity
War, either after the Decimation, or directly

00:01:07.250 --> 00:01:08.300
at Thanos's hands.

00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:12.380
At first, it seems that these images simply
pay tribute to the characters who died in

00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:13.380
the previous movie.

00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:15.160
But there might be more going on here.

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:18.020
All the scenes are rendered in black-and-white...and
red.

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:22.820
Symbolically, red can represent life and death,
being easily associated with blood — you

00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:25.040
know, the stuff you need to keep inside your
body to not die.

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:29.000
But this is a trailer for a movie involving
Infinity Stones — and the red one's the

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:29.920
Reality Stone.

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:34.260
There's been tons of speculation that time
travel will factor into the plot of Avengers:

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:34.760
Endgame.

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:39.060
But could these red-hued shots actually signify
that the Reality Stone will be the key to

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:39.900
their resurrection?

00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:43.550
Who needs time travel if you can just ask
the Reality Stone to make some dead people

00:01:43.550 --> 00:01:44.260
alive again?

00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:47.440
We also see Clint Barton bathed in red light
in the trailer.

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:52.180
And we saw the dead Gamora in an orange light
— which many fans speculate to be her living

00:01:52.180 --> 00:01:54.580
inside the Soul Stone — in Infinity War.

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:57.120
Let's put this theory in the "maybe" category.

00:01:57.880 --> 00:02:01.120
The trailer officially kicks off with a shot
of the Statue of Liberty.

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:06.000
While that's usually a powerful symbol in
film, standing for freedom or America in general,

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:09.420
the details surrounding the statue are what
seem to be most significant.

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:13.220
It doesn't take long to realize that something
looks off about the area surrounding Liberty

00:02:13.220 --> 00:02:16.280
Island — it's practically choked with boats
and ships.

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:20.390
For those of you who don't stare lovingly
at photos of Liberty Island every day, you

00:02:20.390 --> 00:02:24.460
may not realize that this isn't something
that happens often...or really ever.

00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:28.510
Compare the shot in the trailer to this photo
of Liberty Island on a typical day.

00:02:28.510 --> 00:02:31.460
As you can see the amount of boats docked
there goes from "a whole lot" to "exactly

00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:32.900
zero." (that’s a ferry not a boat)

00:02:33.220 --> 00:02:36.120
The implication
here seems to be the utter desolation Thanos's

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:40.060
snap from the end of Avengers: Infinity War
has caused on the rest of the world.

00:02:40.220 --> 00:02:44.800
People are fleeing their home cities — now
probably rendered completely broken due to

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:49.680
half the population blinking out of existence
— and are heading towards New York for refuge.

00:02:49.810 --> 00:02:53.820
The image we get in the trailer makes sense,
too, since the decimation probably destroyed

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:55.180
air travel throughout the world.

00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:58.420
Half the world's pilots and air traffic controllers
are all gone, right?

00:02:58.420 --> 00:03:02.920
So instead, people are getting on boats and
sailing toward the most easily identifiable

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:08.220
port of entry they can find: the giant green
lady holding a torch right outside of Manhattan.

00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:12.540
In a brief but significant moment from the
trailer, we got to see Tony Stark and Nebula

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:14.520
working together on...something.

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:18.130
The last time we saw those two, it seemed
like they were stranded in space together

00:03:18.130 --> 00:03:19.120
on a spaceship.

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:23.700
Stark was recording a sad message for his
lady love, Pepper Potts, while Nebula was

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:25.000
just creeping through the ship.

00:03:25.450 --> 00:03:29.780
Now we get a clear glimpse of the two of them
side-by-side, working on...well, again, we

00:03:29.780 --> 00:03:31.970
still don't know what it is, since it's out
of frame.

00:03:31.970 --> 00:03:33.440
But we can hazard a few guesses.

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:37.320
It could be repairs to the ship, which would
enable it to get wherever they're going more

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:37.820
quickly.

00:03:37.820 --> 00:03:41.480
Maybe they're working on fixing up a suit
of Iron Man armor, which got busted in the

00:03:41.480 --> 00:03:42.819
fight against Thanos.

00:03:42.820 --> 00:03:44.660
Or maybe it's some other thing we don't know
about!

00:03:44.860 --> 00:03:48.770
Either way, the most important revelation
here is that we know for sure that Stark and

00:03:48.770 --> 00:03:53.349
Nebula are putting their skills together to
figure out how not to die in the cold, cold

00:03:53.349 --> 00:03:54.480
vacuum of space.

00:03:55.100 --> 00:03:58.940
There are a couple scenes in the trailer that
are conspicuous by what we don't see.

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:02.980
As we know by now, movie trailers may show
us previews of what we'll see when the movie

00:04:02.980 --> 00:04:03.560
arrives.

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:07.460
But they'll just as often fudge the scenes
they depict in order to keep surprises at

00:04:07.460 --> 00:04:07.960
a maximum.

00:04:08.180 --> 00:04:12.160
The trailer for Infinity War showed Hulk running
behind the rest of the Avengers in Wakanda...and

00:04:12.170 --> 00:04:16.470
we all know now that Hulk never showed up
during that fight scene in the finished film.

00:04:16.470 --> 00:04:19.540
The new trailer for Endgame could be pulling
the same switcheroo.

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:24.160
Early in the trailer, we see a shot of Black
Widow, Captain America, Bruce Banner, and

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:29.030
War Machine walking outside of Avengers headquarters
— with a big old blank space where a fifth

00:04:29.030 --> 00:04:31.080
character would seem to fit easily.

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:36.060
Another shot later shows the Avengers in profile,
lined up — with yet another space for an

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:37.620
apparently missing character.

00:04:37.960 --> 00:04:42.220
If we assume that this is an example of Marvel
Studios misdirecting our attention to keep

00:04:42.220 --> 00:04:45.300
certain spoilers off-screen, who could these
missing characters be?

00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:50.320
If we believe these open spaces point to some
hidden characters, there are a few distinct

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:53.260
possibilities for who Marvel might be keeping
under wraps.

00:04:53.550 --> 00:04:57.460
We know that Hawkeye is back in action in
Endgame, as he shows up at another point in

00:04:57.470 --> 00:05:01.070
the trailer, so it seems unlikely that he'd
have been erased from these scenes.

00:05:01.070 --> 00:05:05.080
But we still haven't caught sight of the Incredible
Hulk — even though we have seen Banner.

00:05:05.410 --> 00:05:08.900
Could the Hulk have been separated from Banner
in this movie, which has happened before in

00:05:08.900 --> 00:05:09.460
the comics?

00:05:09.930 --> 00:05:14.280
We also know that Gwyneth Paltrow will be
in Avengers Endgame to reprise her role as

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:14.780
Pepper Potts.

00:05:14.780 --> 00:05:19.120
It's not crazy to believe that Potts will
take on the role of Rescue, an identity she's

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:22.880
taken on in the comics while wearing one of
Stark's suits of Iron Man armor.

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:27.800
Seeing Potts in an Avengers group shot would
be a huge spoiler, so it makes sense that

00:05:27.810 --> 00:05:30.610
Marvel might want to keep that particular
plot beat under its hat.

00:05:30.610 --> 00:05:34.060
However, there's an even likelier person to
have been erased from the trailer...

00:05:34.940 --> 00:05:39.620
It's very, very likely that the missing hero
is Captain Marvel, whose debut movie is set

00:05:39.620 --> 00:05:42.620
to hit theaters about a month before Avengers:
Endgame drops.

00:05:42.840 --> 00:05:46.680
We know from the end of Infinity War that
Captain Marvel will be important in this new

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:51.060
film, since her logo appears on Nick Fury's
pager just after he gets dusted.

00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:53.960
But what other presence does Captain Marvel
have in this new trailer?

00:05:54.380 --> 00:05:56.700
Surprisingly, the answer is lobsters.

00:05:56.700 --> 00:05:58.120
Yes, that's right: lobsters.

00:05:58.260 --> 00:06:02.180
In one of the few shots featuring Rocket,
the only surviving member of the Guardians

00:06:02.180 --> 00:06:06.860
of the Galaxy, seems to reveal a couple of
interesting details about his possible location.

00:06:07.020 --> 00:06:11.360
He opens up an old wooden door, with a shot
of a rocky shore behind him, along with a

00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:12.740
couple of lobster traps.

00:06:12.940 --> 00:06:13.700
So what, right?

00:06:13.980 --> 00:06:17.790
Well, most of the country's lobsters come
from Maine...and according to The Life of

00:06:17.790 --> 00:06:23.139
Captain Marvel comic book miniseries from
2018, Carol Danvers considers Harpswell, Maine,

00:06:23.140 --> 00:06:24.320
her home away from home.

00:06:24.690 --> 00:06:29.260
Meanwhile, the character's fictional biography
has her born in Boston, a New England location

00:06:29.270 --> 00:06:32.410
that could certainly have a few lobster traps
lying around as well.

00:06:32.410 --> 00:06:36.729
In either case, when you add in the fact that
Captain Marvel is a spacefaring hero, and

00:06:36.729 --> 00:06:41.009
Rocket the alien raccoon seems to be the character
hanging out on the beach, it seems clear that

00:06:41.009 --> 00:06:44.560
Marvel's hinting at Captain Marvel's impending
arrival to save the day.

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:48.540
Thor's whereabouts leave us with a lot more
guesswork than Rocket's.

00:06:48.550 --> 00:06:52.520
We only get a couple of solo shots of the
Thunderer in the trailer: one's a close-up

00:06:52.520 --> 00:06:56.020
of his face, while the next shot shows him
in silhouette at the mouth of some sort of

00:06:56.020 --> 00:06:57.160
cave or structure.

00:06:57.540 --> 00:07:01.300
In the distance we see the green of trees,
and what looks like a hill or a mountain.

00:07:01.640 --> 00:07:05.100
The cave mouth itself seems unnatural, and
potentially man-made.

00:07:05.100 --> 00:07:06.540
So...where is he?

00:07:06.870 --> 00:07:08.260
There are a number of possibilities.

00:07:08.260 --> 00:07:12.800
But like guessing what Stark and Nebula are
up to, all we can do is speculate wildly.

00:07:13.170 --> 00:07:15.940
The colors of the landscape seem to look a
bit like Wakanda.

00:07:15.940 --> 00:07:20.060
The cave, likewise, could be some kind of
base of operations for the Dora Milaje — we

00:07:20.320 --> 00:07:25.370
know Okoye was left alive at the end of Infinity
War, even if the continued existence of T'Challa's

00:07:25.370 --> 00:07:27.419
sister, Shuri, is in doubt.

00:07:27.419 --> 00:07:31.440
Perhaps, however, this shot comes from wherever
Thanos ended up on at the end of Infinity

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:31.940
War.

00:07:31.940 --> 00:07:36.460
The cave mouth — or maybe spaceship — doesn't
seem familiar at first glance, and we don't

00:07:36.470 --> 00:07:38.759
yet know enough about where Thanos retired.

00:07:38.759 --> 00:07:42.820
But plot-wise, it makes a lot of sense for
Thor to visit his former foe, so he could

00:07:42.820 --> 00:07:46.180
retrieve the Infinity Gauntlet and undo Thanos's
damage.

00:07:46.360 --> 00:07:50.560
Of course, we know that it got fried right
after the snap in the last movie, so the sad

00:07:50.560 --> 00:07:52.600
look on his face could indicate a dead end.

00:07:53.180 --> 00:07:57.500
When James "War Machine" Rhodes and Scott
"Ant-Man" Lang last met, it was as enemies

00:07:57.500 --> 00:07:59.120
in Captain America: Civil War.

00:07:59.400 --> 00:08:03.680
The two characters took up opposite sides
of the superheroic conflict, and we've never

00:08:03.720 --> 00:08:06.160
seen them interact as anything other than
enemies.

00:08:06.160 --> 00:08:10.020
In fact, Ant-Man and Captain America basically
threw a truck filled with gasoline at the

00:08:10.020 --> 00:08:10.520
guy.

00:08:10.900 --> 00:08:12.060
"Throw it at this.

00:08:12.860 --> 00:08:13.360
Now!"

00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:18.100
"Oh come on!"

00:08:20.520 --> 00:08:24.600
Needless to say, the two of them didn't have
the kind of introduction that makes for friendship.

00:08:24.610 --> 00:08:28.560
However, we know for sure that both characters
will have important parts to play in Endgame

00:08:28.560 --> 00:08:32.770
— especially when Ant-Man drives up in his
Ant-Van at the end of the first trailer Marvel

00:08:32.770 --> 00:08:34.120
Studios released for the film.

00:08:34.370 --> 00:08:38.160
The new trailer doesn't give us much, but
we do see the pair standing side-by-side,

00:08:38.160 --> 00:08:39.900
getting suited up and ready for action.

00:08:39.969 --> 00:08:43.900
It stands to reason that whatever beef these
two had with each other has been sufficiently

00:08:43.909 --> 00:08:48.190
squashed — at least until reality and/or
the timeline gets reset by the end of the

00:08:48.190 --> 00:08:48.680
movie.

00:08:49.720 --> 00:08:53.140
Captain America is pretty inextricably linked
to his famous shield.

00:08:53.240 --> 00:08:56.980
But believe it or not, he hasn't held the
thing up since the end of Captain America:

00:08:56.980 --> 00:08:57.560
Civil War.

00:08:57.760 --> 00:09:02.180
He was rocking a different shield entirely
in Infinity War — sort of a triangular,

00:09:02.200 --> 00:09:05.000
black buckler that was provided by T'Challa
in Wakanda.

00:09:05.230 --> 00:09:09.000
But in this new trailer for Endgame, we get
a shot of what seems to be the inside of Captain

00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:10.360
America's classic shield.

00:09:10.660 --> 00:09:14.040
The circumstances of him reclaiming this weapon
are a big question mark.

00:09:14.040 --> 00:09:18.160
Last time we saw it was when he threw it at
Tony Stark's feet after the two of them finished

00:09:18.160 --> 00:09:19.500
fighting in Civil War.

00:09:19.700 --> 00:09:20.880
"My father made that shield."

00:09:28.900 --> 00:09:31.740
Maybe Cap swung by Tony's empty penthouse
to pick it up?

00:09:31.980 --> 00:09:35.600
Or maybe this is yet more grist for the mill
when it comes to the constant time travel

00:09:35.600 --> 00:09:37.900
theories that continue to follow this movie.

00:09:38.180 --> 00:09:41.980
Regardless of how he gets his hands on it,
everyone knows just one thing:

00:09:42.340 --> 00:09:49.380
When Captain America throws his mighty shield,
all those who chose to oppose his shield must

00:09:49.380 --> 00:09:51.160
yield!"

00:09:51.660 --> 00:09:53.980
